                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran examines the structural tension between scaling and profitability in venture capital, arguing that many startups prioritize revenue growth over sustainable business models. He analyzes how founder incentives, investor preferences, and shifts in capital markets have deepened this imbalance. Private companies now scale significantly before going public while remaining unprofitable, pushing the reckoning onto public market investors. The post challenges VCs to reconcile their enthusiasm for scale with the basic requirements of profitable business survival.",
                                                              "take":  "The VC industry\u0027s tolerance for indefinite unprofitability is not just a style choice ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â it is a structural incentive problem that public markets are left to resolve. For sophisticated investors, this post is a reminder that IPO pricing for late-stage VC-backed companies often embeds the optimistic scaling narrative while discounting the profitability gap. The smarter posture is to demand a credible path to unit economics before assigning premium multiples, regardless of how compelling the growth story sounds at the roadshow.",
