                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown argues that current computing infrastructure is dramatically underprepared for the coming era of AI agents at scale. Citing expert Alex Kantrowitz, he posits that when billions of people gain personal AI assistants, demand will vastly exceed today\u0027s available compute capacity. Brown contends that Wall Street underestimates future infrastructure needs by focusing on today\u0027s chatbot workloads rather than the far heavier compute demands of autonomous vehicles, robots, and continuously-running AI agents.",
                                                                                "take":  "Brown\u0027s argument is directionally correct and the implication is underappreciated in current market pricing: the shift from AI as an interactive tool to AI as an autonomous agent represents a non-linear jump in compute demand. A chatbot session lasts minutes; a persistent personal AI agent runs 24/7. This demand profile change is not yet priced into infrastructure stock valuations using today\u0027s utilization assumptions. For investors and enterprise operators alike, the practical takeaway is to think about AI infrastructure spend not as a capital cycle that peaks and corrects but as a structural buildout analogous to electrification ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the endpoint demand is genuinely difficult to model from today\u0027s starting point.",
