                                                                                "date":  "2026-08-17",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  null,
                                                                                "summary":  "Santoli examines the S\u0026P 500\u0027s 6%-plus rally over 12 trading days following late-July weakness, characterizing it as a rebound that carried the index to a new record. He raises quality concerns about the earnings driving the record: results were partly flattered by tech giants marking up their stakes in OpenAI and Anthropic, data-center buildout revenue that is essentially hyperscalers recycling their own past earnings, a one-time energy-sector earnings surge tied to wartime supply disruptions, and companies lapping tough second-quarter 2025 comparables. Earnings forecasts continue to rise, suggesting the moment of reckoning is not immediate, but the underlying quality of reported results is weaker than headline numbers suggest.",
                                                                                "take":  "Santoli is raising an important accounting-reality issue that deserves more attention: when tech giants book unrealized gains on their OpenAI and Anthropic stakes as earnings, those figures inflate reported profitability without any cash generation. Combined with data-center revenue that is essentially a circular transfer between hyperscalers, a meaningful portion of 2026\u0027s earnings \u0027bonanza\u0027 reflects financial engineering and accounting conventions rather than genuine underlying economic activity. Investors should look at free cash flow and strip out the stake-valuation gains before trusting the headline earnings picture that drove this rally.",
